    "text": "Outcome:"
  },
  {
    "start": 124,
    "end": 127,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 129,
    "end": 133,
    "text": "Calculate private, public, and national saving in"
  },
  {
    "start": 134,
    "end": 138,
    "text": "closed and open economies."
  },
  {
    "start": 139,
    "end": 142,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 143,
    "end": 147,
    "text": "Difficulty:"
  },
  {
    "start": 172,
    "end": 175,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 176,
    "end": 180,
    "text": "Intermediate"
  },
  {
    "start": 182,
    "end": 185,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 186,
    "end": 190,
    "text": "Saving, Investment, and National-Saving Identities"
  },
  {
    "start": 200,
    "end": 203,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 204,
    "end": 208,
    "text": "+"
  },
  {
    "start": 210,
    "end": 213,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 214,
    "end": 218,
    "text": "THE CORE IDEA"
  },
  {
    "start": 225,
    "end": 228,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 230,
    "end": 234,
    "text": "With net taxes T, private saving is Y - T - C and public saving is T - G. National saving is their sum: S = Y - C - G. In a closed economy, S"
  },
  {
    "start": 235,
    "end": 239,
    "text": "= I. In an open economy, S = I + NCO; because NX = NCO in the course model, S = I + NX. These are aggregate identities, not claims"
  },
  {
    "start": 240,
    "end": 244,
    "text": "that each saver directly funds one investor."
  },
  {
    "start": 254,
    "end": 257,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 258,
    "end": 262,
    "text": "?"
  },
  {
    "start": 264,
    "end": 267,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 268,
    "end": 272,
    "text": "HOW TO RECOGNIZE IT"
  },
  {
    "start": 279,
    "end": 282,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 283,
    "end": 287,
    "text": "-"
  },
  {
    "start": 288,
    "end": 292,
    "text": "Budget surplus means T - G is positive; a deficit makes public saving negative."
  },
  {
    "start": 294,
    "end": 297,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 298,
    "end": 302,
    "text": "-"
  },
  {
    "start": 303,
    "end": 307,
    "text": "Adding private and public saving cancels T and gives Y - C - G."
  },
  {
    "start": 309,
    "end": 312,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 313,
    "end": 317,
    "text": "-"
  },
  {
    "start": 318,
    "end": 322,
    "text": "Use S = I only for the closed-economy model."
  },
  {
    "start": 324,
    "end": 327,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 328,
    "end": 332,
    "text": "-"
  },
  {
    "start": 333,
    "end": 337,
    "text": "In an open economy, saving can finance domestic investment or net capital outflow."
  },
  {
    "start": 362,
    "end": 365,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 366,
    "end": 370,
    "text": "!"
  },
  {
    "start": 372,
    "end": 375,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 376,
    "end": 380,
    "text": "WATCH OUT"
  },
  {
    "start": 381,
    "end": 384,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 386,
    "end": 390,
    "text": "Do not reverse the public-saving sign. T - G is public saving; G - T is the budget deficit. Also state the closed-economy assumption"
  },
  {
    "start": 391,
    "end": 395,
    "text": "before writing S = I."
  },
  {
    "start": 420,
    "end": 423,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 424,
    "end": 428,
    "text": "#"
  },
  {
    "start": 430,
    "end": 433,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 434,
    "end": 438,
    "text": "WORKED EXAMPLE: FROM COMPONENTS TO OPEN-ECONOMY SAVING"
  },
  {
    "start": 455,
    "end": 458,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 459,
    "end": 463,
    "text": "KEY RELATIONSHIPS"
  },
  {
    "start": 472,
    "end": 475,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 477,
    "end": 481,
    "text": "Private saving = Y - T - C"
  },
  {
    "start": 490,
    "end": 493,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 495,
    "end": 499,
    "text": "Public saving = T - G"
  },
  {
    "start": 508,
    "end": 511,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 513,
    "end": 517,
    "text": "National saving = Y - C - G"
  },
  {
    "start": 526,
    "end": 529,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 531,
    "end": 535,
    "text": "Closed: S = I"
  },
  {
    "start": 544,
    "end": 547,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 549,
    "end": 553,
    "text": "Open: S = I + NCO; NX = NCO"
  },
  {
    "start": 554,
    "end": 557,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 559,
    "end": 563,
    "text": "Let Y = $1,200, T = $150, C = $760, and G = $260. Private saving is $290;"
  },
  {
    "start": 564,
    "end": 568,
    "text": "public saving is -$110; national saving is $180. If domestic investment is"
  },
  {
    "start": 569,
    "end": 573,
    "text": "$140, then NCO = S - I = $40. The accounting identity also gives NX = $40."
  },
  {
    "start": 598,
    "end": 601,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 602,
    "end": 606,
    "text": "?"
  },
  {
    "start": 608,
    "end": 611,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 612,
    "end": 616,
    "text": "CHECK YOURSELF"
  },
  {
    "start": 617,
    "end": 620,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 622,
    "end": 626,
    "text": "Private saving is $220 and public saving is -$50. What is national saving?"
  },
  {
    "start": 641,
    "end": 644,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
    "start": 645,
    "end": 649,
    "text": "READY?"
  },
  {
    "start": 651,
    "end": 654,
    "text": ""
  },
  {
